WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.852
Task statement 4.5.

2
00:00:01.852 --> 00:00:05.053
Design efficient batch processing strategies.

3
00:00:05.053 --> 00:00:14.303
A nightly audit of 40,000 documents costs half as much as a batch; a pre-merge check that blocks developers must not wait for one.

4
00:00:15.703 --> 00:00:21.211
The Message Batches API lets you send a large set of requests and collect the results later.

5
00:00:21.211 --> 00:00:29.353
It costs half the normal price, and results arrive within twenty four hours, often much sooner, but with no promise for any single item.

6
00:00:29.353 --> 00:00:36.119
It is like an overnight laundry service: cheaper, but you would not use it for the shirt you need in ten minutes.

7
00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:42.710
Every request in a batch has a custom ID that you set, such as the document ID.

8
00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:47.179
Results can come back in any order, so always match them by that ID.

9
00:00:47.179 --> 00:00:55.460
Each request is a single model turn, so an agent that needs to call tools and continue cannot run its loop inside one request.

10
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:59.535
Each result is marked succeeded, errored, canceled or expired.

11
00:01:00.935 --> 00:01:05.917
Batch work that nobody is waiting on, such as overnight audits or weekly reports.

12
00:01:05.917 --> 00:01:08.807
Keep anything that blocks a person synchronous.

13
00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:16.434
Before sending thousands of requests, test the prompt on a small sample, because a bad prompt in a batch wastes a whole run.

14
00:01:16.434 --> 00:01:23.015
When some requests fail, fix the cause, such as splitting oversized documents, and resubmit only those IDs.

15
00:01:24.415 --> 00:01:31.037
The extraction team pilots fifty documents first and fixes two prompt problems before spending money at scale.

16
00:01:31.037 --> 00:01:35.252
The full set goes overnight, each request tagged with its document ID.

17
00:01:35.252 --> 00:01:41.694
A few hundred fail because they are too long, so the team splits those into chunks and resubmits only them.

18
00:01:41.694 --> 00:01:46.089
Meanwhile, the check developers wait on before merging stays synchronous.

19
00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:49.661
Batch questions usually test fit.

20
00:01:49.661 --> 00:01:54.135
If a person is waiting, do not batch, however tempting the discount.

21
00:01:54.135 --> 00:01:57.425
If a few requests fail, fix and resend just those.

22
00:01:57.425 --> 00:01:59.202
Never rely on result order.

23
00:01:59.202 --> 00:02:06.967
And remember that a batch request is one turn, so an agent loop that needs to execute tools does not belong inside it.

24
00:02:08.367 --> 00:02:11.384
The Message Batches API trades speed for cost.

25
00:02:11.384 --> 00:02:17.680
Use it for work that can wait, tag every request with a custom ID, and match results on that ID.

26
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:19.910
Test the prompt on a sample first.

27
00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.911
When some requests fail, fix the cause and resend just those.

28
00:02:23.911 --> 00:02:26.863
Anything blocking a person stays synchronous.

